> - **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
>   period contains that time validates it**. A KSK's reliance period runs from the start of its signing period to the
>   end of its signing period plus the maximum validity of the root DNSKEY RRSIGs it made, recorded with its source:
>   evidence it signed while signing stays usable for that long and no longer (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13, after
>   `docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F2). The rule is existential and order-independent: a candidate that fails is
>   informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible key has been tried (F1). A trusted KSK that
>   is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure. The trust configuration records signing and
>   reliance periods separately from publication and revocation dates.
> - **Trust configuration is append-only** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13). KSKs, header pins and repository keys are never
>   removed, and a closed period never changes. The repository key carries a signing period and is evaluated by the
>   same existential rule as the KSKs, so it can be replaced without failing old receipts or trusting a retired key
>   forever. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
